Apps are built with Wrangler and deployed to Cloudflare Workers. Each app declares its bindings (D1, R2, KV), non-secret `vars` and runtime secrets in its `wrangler.jsonc`.

{{#if (has "stack" "nextjs")}}
### Next.js (OpenNext)

**Build & deploy**
{{#if (isMono)}}
`build:cf`, `preview` and `deploy` are per-app scripts: run them from the app directory (`cd apps/<app>`). Next.js apps: {{> cloudflare/app-list stack="nextjs"}}.

{{/if}}
- `{{runAppScript "build:cf"}}` — binding types → OpenNext adapter build (it runs the `build` script, `next build`, once itself)
- `{{runAppScript "preview"}}` — local Wrangler preview of the built output
- `{{runAppScript "deploy"}}` — deploy the built output to Cloudflare Workers
- `{{runScript "cf-typegen"}}` — regenerate `cloudflare-env.d.ts` from `wrangler.jsonc`. Install runs it (`postinstall`); run it again after every change to bindings

There is no `start` script: `next start` runs Next's Node server, where `getCloudflareContext()` is never initialised, so every page returns a 500. Run `{{runAppScript "build:cf"}}` then `{{runAppScript "preview"}}` to serve the production build in workerd.

**Local dev**: `{{runScript "dev"}}` runs `next dev` with `initOpenNextCloudflareForDev()` active (wired in `next.config.ts`, development only), making Cloudflare bindings available through `wrangler.jsonc`.

**Environment variables and secrets**

1. **Build time** (`next build`): values from `.env` are inlined. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` are frozen into client JS here.
2. **`{{runAppScript "build:cf"}}`**: OpenNext bundles every `.env*` value (all modes, server secrets included) into the Worker, in `.open-next/cloudflare/next-env.mjs`, as a `process.env` fallback. There is no opt-out. A secret set with `wrangler secret put` wins at runtime, but the bundled value is silently used when the secret was never set.
3. **Runtime** (the deployed Worker): `wrangler.jsonc` `vars` and secrets. Wrangler does not load `.env` here: OpenNext turns that off and handles `.env` itself, through the bundle.

The runtime secrets of the app are listed in `wrangler.jsonc` under `secrets.required`, generated from the variables the project marks as secrets: a deploy fails until each is set, and `{{runAppScript "preview"}}` warns about a missing one. That preview warning also appears when the secret is in `.env`, because Wrangler does not read it; it is safe to ignore then.

**Where each kind of value lives**

| Kind | Local | Production | Read via |
|------|-------|------------|----------|
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` | `.env` | shell or CI env of the build | baked into client bundle |
| Server config | `.env` or `wrangler.jsonc vars` | `wrangler.jsonc vars` | `process.env.X` |
| Secret | `.env` (gitignored, development values only) | `wrangler secret put X`, or `--secrets-file .prod.env` on the first deploy | `process.env.X` |
| Binding (D1/R2/KV) | `wrangler.jsonc` + local persistence | `wrangler.jsonc` | `getCloudflareContext().env.X` **only** |

**Key rules**
- Build the Worker that you deploy where `.env` holds no real secret (CI, or a checkout without a `.env`). A build run next to a `.env` with production values ships them inside the Worker.
- Set production secrets with `{{#inDirectory "apps/<app>"}}{{installedBinaryRunner}} wrangler secret put X{{/inDirectory}}`. A new Worker does not exist yet, so its first deploy takes them from a file: `{{#inDirectory "apps/<app>"}}{{runScript "deploy"}} -- --secrets-file .prod.env{{/inDirectory}}` (gitignored).{{#if (hasLibrary "better-auth")}} `BETTER_AUTH_URL` is the production URL of the Worker, which Wrangler only prints on deploy: see Production URL below to learn it first.{{/if}}
- `{{runAppScript "preview"}}` serves the build as it is: the values of `.env` are frozen when `{{runAppScript "build:cf"}}` runs, so run it again after every `.env` change.
- Keep local secrets in `.env`. Do NOT add `.dev.vars`: Next.js and OpenNext read `.env`, and a second file would hold a second copy of each secret.
- `NODE_ENV=production` is set in `wrangler.jsonc` `vars`. The Next.js build only replaces the literal `process.env.NODE_ENV` in bundled code; dependencies and dynamic reads see the Worker's real variable, which reads `development` in workerd without the var. `wrangler dev`, `preview` and `deploy` run the built output and carry the var; `next dev` keeps `NODE_ENV=development`.
- Bindings are never on `process.env` — always use `getCloudflareContext().env`.
{{#if (or (has "database" "d1") (databaseBinding "hyperdrive"))}}
- `getEnv()` (`src/lib/env.ts`) calls `connection()` before reading the context, so `next build` never prerenders a page that reads a binding: during a build the context comes from a local workerd started per build worker, and several of them opening the same local D1 fail with `SQLITE_BUSY`. A page that must read a binding at build time cannot go through `getEnv()`.
{{/if}}
- Do NOT name the prod-secrets file `.env.production` — Next.js inlines it at build and would leak secrets.
{{#if (hasLibrary "trpc")}}
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` must be the production URL when the Worker you deploy is built: Next.js freezes it into the client bundle and the server code, and the server-side tRPC client calls the app at it (`getAppUrl()`), so a build that left the local value deploys a Worker that calls `localhost`. Export it in the shell of that build, then build: `export NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://<worker name>.<subdomain>.workers.dev`, then `{{runAppScript "build:cf"}}`. The Production URL notes below teach how to learn that URL before the first build.
{{/if}}

